  <xacro:tubo x1="${0}" y1="${0}" z1="${0}" x2="${(0.0509408)+(0.0828882)+(0.0293545)+(0)}" y2="${(0.0161893)+(0.0000000)+(0.0275215)+(0.0210457)}" z2="${(rk_r7)+(rk_r9)+(rk_r11)+(-bogie_drop)}" r="0.025"/>
  <xacro:tubo x1="${0}" y1="${0}" z1="${0}" x2="${(-0.0509408)+(-0.246072)+(-0.0707323)}" y2="${(0.0161893)+(0.0000000)+(0.0000000)}" z2="${(-0.053192)+(rk_front_z)+(rk_p74)}" r="0.025"/>
  <xacro:tubo x1="${((-0.0509408)+(-0.246072)+(-0.0707323))*AcopleTuboRB_1_1__t}" y1="${((0.0161893)+(0.0000000)+(0.0000000))*AcopleTuboRB_1_1__t}" z1="${((-0.053192)+(rk_front_z)+(rk_p74))*AcopleTuboRB_1_1__t}" x2="${-0.0509408}" y2="${0.0161893}" z2="${-0.053192}" r="0.014"/>
  <!-- BRAZO DEL DIFERENCIAL.  Sube del eje del rocker a la altura
       de la viga para que la barra salga horizontal.  Va RIGIDO con
       el rocker -no lleva junta- asi que no anade cuerpo ni grado
       de libertad; lo unico que hace la punta es llevar la junta de
       bola 96.808 mm arriba.  Sale a y = +-0.1335592 del centro del
       vehiculo, o sea 37 mm POR FUERA del costado del chasis
       (+-0.0963): no lo atraviesa. -->
  <xacro:if value="${diff_brazo}">
    <xacro:tubo x1="${0}" y1="${0.0161893}" z1="${0}"
                x2="${0}" y2="${0.0161893}" z2="${0.096808}" r="0.012"/>
  </xacro:if>
</link>

<!-- ================= ROCKER PIVOTS (passive suspension) ================= -->
<!--
  SUSPENSION TOPOLOGY  (restructured 2026-07 - see robot_metrics/README.md)

  What was wrong before
  =====================
  The two sides of the suspension were welded to each other.  The chain was

      base_link
        `- differential_joint (revolute Y)  -> AcopleTuboRB_1_1   [left tube]
             `- differential_bar_joint (FIXED) -> AcopleTuboRB_2_1 [right tube]

  so AcopleTuboRB_1_1 and AcopleTuboRB_2_1 formed ONE rigid body carrying every
  wheel on both sides, connected to the chassis by a single pivot.  The model
  therefore had no independent left/right rocker travel whatsoever: when the
  left wheels met the step, the right wheels were forced to follow.  That is the
  opposite of what a rocker-bogie does, and it is the direct cause of the
  wheel lift-off seen when crossing the step.

  The joints actually named "rocker_joint_r" and "rocker_joint_l" were not in
  the load path at all: their children (AcopleRB_1_1 / AcopleRB_2_1) are leaf
  links that carry no wheel, so those revolute joints only wobbled two
  cosmetic stubs.  They are now fixed (see below).

  What the structure is now
  =========================
      base_link
        |- rocker_pivot_left  (revolute Y) -> AcopleTuboRB_1_1  [LEFT rocker]
        `- rocker_pivot_right (revolute Y) -> AcopleTuboRB_2_1  [RIGHT rocker]

  Both pivots share the same axis line (same x and z, axis +Y), 0.337582 m
  apart in y, so they form a proper coaxial rocker pair.  Each rocker carries,
  on its own side, the classic rocker-bogie arrangement:
      * the FRONT wheel, mounted directly on the rocker through a steering
        joint;
      * a BOGIE at the other end (rev_3 / rev_4) carrying the MIDDLE and REAR
        wheels, the rear one also steered.

  Sign convention - this is easy to get wrong and was got wrong before.
  base_link is authored facing -x (the camera sits at x = -0.07 with rpy
  yaw = pi, and the robot spawns at Y = pi).  With z up and a right-handed
  frame, LEFT = z_hat x forward = z_hat x (-x_hat) = -y_hat.  So in base_link
  coordinates the LEFT side is the one with the SMALLER y:

      AcopleTuboRB_2_1  pivot at y = -0.052384  -> LEFT  rocker
      AcopleTuboRB_1_1  pivot at y = +0.285198  -> RIGHT rocker

  base_link's origin comes from the CAD export and is not centred on the
  chassis, which is why the two offsets are not symmetric about zero; the
  centreline is y = +0.116408 (where the sensors sit) and the pivot separation
  (0.337582 m) is what matters.

  Travel limits
  =============
  The left pivot previously had limits of +/-0.15 rad.  Lifting one wheel over
  the 0.10 m step needs about atan(0.10 / 0.285) = 0.34 rad of rocker rotation,
  so the old limit made it geometrically IMPOSSIBLE for the suspension to
  absorb the step - it hit the joint stop and the chassis was levered upward
  instead.  Limits are now +/-0.6 rad (+/-34 deg), which clears the step with
  margin and is consistent with the hard stops of a physical rocker-bogie.

  Damping and friction
  ====================
  Previously damping = 80 N*m*s/rad and friction = 4-6 N*m on a passive pivot,
  i.e. the suspension was effectively frozen.  Values are now 0.5 and 0.2,
  representative of a lubricated bearing on this scale.  These two numbers are
  the ones to vary in a sensitivity study.

  Actuation
  =========
  These pivots are PASSIVE.  They carry an EffortJointInterface transmission
  (see ensamblajeurdf.trans) whose only purpose is to let rocker_differential.py
  apply the differential coupling torque.  With that node stopped the effort
  command stays at zero and the joints are free - which is exactly the
  "no differential" ablation.  They are never position- or velocity-driven.
-->
<!-- ==================== SUSPENSION TRAVEL ====================
     The two rocker pivots below travel +-1.0 rad (set again 2026-09-13;
     the bogies stay at -0.6 / bogie_upper, see the top of this file).  They were +-0.6 rad, sized against the
     0.10 m step obstacle alone: "0.10 m step over a 0.285 m half-span needs
     ~0.34 rad" - true, but the step is not the demanding case.  On the mine
     route the gallery climbs about 5 m, and both rocker pivots were measured
     PINNED against the +0.6 rad stop for minutes at a time (sd 0.25 deg),
     with the chassis riding at a mean pitch of -27 deg where the terrain grade
     was only about 7 deg.  A suspension at its stop does not articulate: it
     transmits terrain pitch straight into the chassis, which is the opposite
     of the property this study is measuring.

     +-1.0 rad comes from the criterion that DEFINES the mechanism - all six
     wheels stay in ground contact while surmounting an obstacle of one wheel
     diameter (MER is specified at roughly 1.5 diameters; one is conservative).
     With this model's measured geometry:

         wheel diameter D        = 0.356 m
         rocker front lever arm  = 0.4266 m   front wheel to rocker pivot
         bogie half-span         = 0.2133 m   middle wheel to bogie pivot

         rocker: asin(D / 0.4266)       = 0.988 rad = 56.6 deg
         bogie : asin(D / (2 x 0.2133)) = 0.988 rad = 56.6 deg

     Both round to 1.0 rad, which is what is set here.  At the old +-0.6 rad the
     same criterion admitted only a 0.24 m obstacle - two thirds of a wheel.

     STILL TO CHECK: whether the CAD linkage actually clears itself at 1.0 rad.
     The meshes are a CAD export and self_collide is false on the wheels, so
     Gazebo will not complain about interpenetration - look at it in the GUI
     before trusting a contact result.
     ============================================================ -->

<!-- ================= SUSPENSION DAMPING ================= -->
<!--
  damping = 5.0 N*m*s/rad on the four PASSIVE suspension joints
  (rocker_pivot_left, rocker_pivot_right, rev_3, rev_4), set 2026-09-08.
  History of this number: 80.0 in the original GitHub model (2026-03-12),
  0.5 for most of this study, then 80.0, 0.1 and 5.0 on 2026-09-08 as the
  suspension was being swept by hand.

  WHAT THE SWEEP IS FOR.  Damping trades ground contact for ride
  smoothness on this platform, and the two points measured over the full
  mine route (robot_metrics/ROCKER_RUEDAS_AL_AIRE.md section 2) are the
  ends of the range, not this value:

                           damping 0.5     damping 80
      vibration RMS            2.281          1.693
      az_max                 160.9           53.3
      peak |a|               365            123
      time with <6 wheels     19.7 %         51.7 %
      Rueda_4_1 airborne       6.4 %         38.0 %

  5.0 is an INTERIOR point of that pair and nothing here says where it
  lands.  The two ends differ by a factor of 160 and the response is not
  known to be linear in damping, so do not read a proportion off the
  table.  If 5.0 is meant to be the compromise between the smooth ride of
  80 and the ground contact of 0.5, that claim needs its own run: the two
  numbers to log are vibration RMS and time with fewer than six wheels,
  and they are the two that move in opposite directions.

  friction stays at 0.2 and has not moved through any of these changes.
  At 5.0 the viscous term is back in charge: 5.0 * qdot outweighs the
  0.2 N*m of Coulomb friction above qdot = 0.04 rad/s, which the
  suspension is above whenever it is doing anything at all.  That was not
  true at damping 0.1, where friction was the governing term.

  STABILITY: gazebo-classic integrates joint damping explicitly
  (implicitSpringDamper is not set here), and the explicit form is stable
  while damping < 2*I/dt, i.e. 4000*I at dt = 0.0005 s.  The rocker
  pivots (I ~ 4.05 kg*m^2) are bounded near 16000 and the bogies, the
  lighter pair, near a few hundred.  5.0 is far below both.

  check_sim_parity.py does NOT police these four: the other two platforms
  have no passive suspension to match.  The DRIVE joints (rev_9..rev_14)
  are the policed ones and stay at damping 0.0.
-->

<joint name="rocker_pivot_right" type="revolute">
  <origin xyz="0.244209 0.2177132 0.576664" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="chassis_link"/>
  <child link="AcopleTuboRB_1_1"/>
  <axis xyz="0.0 1.0 0.0"/>
  <!-- +-1.0 rad desde 2026-09-13 (antes +-0.6); ver SUSPENSION TRAVEL. -->
  <limit lower="-1.0" upper="1.0" effort="1000" velocity="10.0"/>
  <dynamics damping="5.0" friction="0.2"/>
</joint>

<joint name="rocker_pivot_left" type="revolute">
  <!--
    Origin expressed in base_link: the old fixed joint placed AcopleTuboRB_2_1
    at (0, -0.337582, 0) in the frame of AcopleTuboRB_1_1, whose own origin was
    (0.283305, 0.285198, 0.576664).  Composing the two at zero deflection gives
    y = 0.285198 - 0.337582 = -0.052384, so the link keeps exactly the pose it
    had before; only its parent and its degree of freedom changed.
  -->
  <origin xyz="0.244209 -0.0170268 0.576664" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="chassis_link"/>
  <child link="AcopleTuboRB_2_1"/>
  <axis xyz="0.0 1.0 0.0"/>
  <!-- +-1.0 rad desde 2026-09-13 (antes +-0.6); ver SUSPENSION TRAVEL. -->
  <limit lower="-1.0" upper="1.0" effort="1000" velocity="10.0"/>
  <dynamics damping="5.0" friction="0.2"/>
</joint>

<!-- ================= PIEZAS FUSIONADAS =================
  Desde 2026-09-13 las cadenas de juntas fijas del CAD (acoples, codos de
  90 y 135, soportes, motores) estan fusionadas en la pieza que articula:
  AcopleTuboRB_* (rocker), Acople_Rodamiento_* (bogie) y BarraRuedas_1/3/4/6
  (direccion).  Gazebo ya las fusionaba al cargar, asi que la fisica es la
  misma; la geometria de juntas tambien.  Cada brazo es un tubo recto. -->












<!-- ================= BOGIE PIVOTS (passive) ================= -->
<!--
  rev_3 = LEFT  bogie  (descends from AcopleTuboRB_2_1 = rocker_pivot_left)
  rev_4 = RIGHT bogie  (descends from AcopleTuboRB_1_1 = rocker_pivot_right)

  Each bogie carries the MIDDLE wheel and the REAR wheel of its side, the rear
  one through a steering joint; the FRONT wheel of that side hangs directly
  off the rocker.  That is the standard rocker-bogie arrangement.  These are
  the joints the reviewer asked about: they are now genuinely passive revolute
  joints.

  Verified passive:
    * no transmission          - removed from ensamblajeurdf.trans, so
                                 gazebo_ros_control never registers them and
                                 cannot command them;
    * no controller            - not listed in any controller_manager spawn;
    * no PID entry             - removed from the launch pid_gains block.

  Previously they DID carry a hardware_interface/VelocityJointInterface
  transmission.  gazebo_ros_control registers every transmitted joint and, in
  every write cycle, drives it towards its command - which for an unclaimed
  joint is a constant zero velocity.  The "passive" bogies were therefore being
  actively held still by the simulator, on top of damping = 80 N*m*s/rad and
  friction = 10 N*m from the URDF.  A bogie that cannot rotate transfers the
  step impulse straight into the chassis and lifts the neighbouring wheel; this
  is the main reason the simulated contact behaviour did not match Fig. 2.

  Limits widened from +/-0.5 to +/-0.6 rad for the same geometric reason as the
  rockers (0.10 m step over a 0.285 m half-span needs ~0.34 rad, and the bogie
  span is shorter still, so the old stop was close to binding).
-->
<joint name="rev_3" type="revolute">
  <origin xyz="${(0.0509408)+(0.0828882)+(0.0293545)+(0)} ${(-0.0161893)+(0.0000000)+(-0.0275215)+(-0.0210457)} ${(rk_r7)+(rk_r9)+(rk_r11)+(-bogie_drop)}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="AcopleTuboRB_2_1"/>
  <child link="Acople_Rodamiento_1_1"/>
  <axis xyz="-0.0 1.0 -0.0"/>
  <limit lower="-0.6" upper="$(arg bogie_upper)" effort="1000" velocity="10.0"/>
  <dynamics damping="5.0" friction="0.2"/>
</joint>

<joint name="rev_4" type="revolute">
  <origin xyz="${(0.0509408)+(0.0828882)+(0.0293545)+(0)} ${(0.0161893)+(0.0000000)+(0.0275215)+(0.0210457)} ${(rk_r7)+(rk_r9)+(rk_r11)+(-bogie_drop)}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="AcopleTuboRB_1_1"/>
  <child link="Acople_Rodamiento_2_1"/>
  <axis xyz="0.0 1.0 0.0"/>
  <limit lower="-0.6" upper="$(arg bogie_upper)" effort="1000" velocity="10.0"/>
  <dynamics damping="5.0" friction="0.2"/>
</joint>











<!-- ================= STEERING LOCK =================
  +-1.1 rad (63 deg), not the +-0.5 rad (28.6 deg) this used to be.

  A rocker-bogie turns on the spot: the four corner wheels steer tangent to a
  circle about the vehicle centre while the two middle wheels, which sit ON
  that centre line, stay straight and are already tangent.  Every Mars rover
  from MER onward does this, and twist_to_wheels.py has always had the
  spin_turn() routine for it - its own comment says the wheels are "turned as
  close to tangent as the steering lock allows", which at 0.5 rad was not close
  at all.

  The angles the manoeuvre needs, computed from this model's own measured wheel
  positions about the centre (0.2833, 0.1164):

      front-left  -60.07 deg    front-right  +60.07 deg
      rear-left   +51.55 deg    rear-right   -51.55 deg

  so 1.0484 rad is the requirement and 1.1 gives a little margin.  The middle
  axle sits at x = 0.2833 against a rotation centre of x = 0.2833, an offset of
  0.0000 m - the geometry was laid out for this manoeuvre, and only the joint
  limit was stopping it.

  What this removes: with a 28.6 deg lock the platform could not achieve any
  turn tighter than a 1.56 m radius, while DWA - which has no notion of a
  minimum radius - was measured commanding radii down to 0.13 m.  The
  difference was scrubbed off sideways by the tyres, which is where the 17-21
  N*m torque peaks came from.

  STILL TO CHECK: whether the CAD steering linkage actually clears itself at
  1.1 rad.  Widening a joint limit without looking is how the suspension travel
  change went wrong earlier - look at it in the GUI.
  ================================================= -->
<joint name="rev_5" type="revolute">
  <origin xyz="${(-0.0509408)+(-0.246072)+(-0.0707323)} ${(-0.0161893)+(0.0000000)+(0.0000000)} ${(-0.053192)+(rk_front_z)+(rk_p74)}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="AcopleTuboRB_2_1"/>
  <child link="BarraRuedas_1_1"/>
  <axis xyz="0 0 1"/>
  <limit lower="-1.1" upper="1.1" effort="100" velocity="1.0"/>
  <dynamics damping="10.0" friction="1.0"/>
</joint>


<joint name="rev_6" type="revolute">
  <origin xyz="${(0.0509408)+(0.0828882)+(0.0707323)} ${(-0.0161893)+(0.0000000)+(0.0000000)} ${(-0.053192 + bogie_drop)+(-0.086551)+(rk_p74)}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="Acople_Rodamiento_1_1"/>
  <child link="BarraRuedas_3_1"/>
  <axis xyz="0 0 1"/>
  <limit lower="-1.1" upper="1.1" effort="100" velocity="1.0"/>
  <dynamics damping="10.0" friction="1.0"/>
</joint>

<joint name="rev_7" type="revolute">
  <origin xyz="${(-0.0509408)+(-0.246072)+(-0.0707323)} ${(0.0161893)+(0.0000000)+(0.0000000)} ${(-0.053192)+(rk_front_z)+(rk_p74)}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="AcopleTuboRB_1_1"/>
  <child link="BarraRuedas_4_1"/>
  <axis xyz="0 0 1"/>
  <limit lower="-1.1" upper="1.1" effort="100" velocity="1.0"/>
  <dynamics damping="10.0" friction="1.0"/>
</joint>


<joint name="rev_8" type="revolute">
  <origin xyz="${(0.0509408)+(0.0828882)+(0.0707323)} ${(0.0161893)+(0.0000000)+(0.0000000)} ${(-0.053192 + bogie_drop)+(-0.086551)+(rk_p74)}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="Acople_Rodamiento_2_1"/>
  <child link="BarraRuedas_6_1"/>
  <axis xyz="0 0 1"/>
  <limit lower="-1.1" upper="1.1" effort="100" velocity="1.0"/>
  <dynamics damping="10.0" friction="1.0"/>
</joint>













<!-- ==================== ACTUATION BUDGET ====================
     The six drive joints below (rev_9 .. rev_14) carry the Rocker-Bogie's
     share of the shared tractive-force budget declared in
     robot_metrics/config/sim_config.yaml:

         tau_max = F_budget * r / n_driven = 810.811 * 0.148 / 6 = 20.000 N*m

     so that the three platforms have the same total drive authority at the
     contact patch (674.2 N) despite driving 4, 2 and 6 joints respectively.
     check_sim_parity.py fails the campaign if this figure drifts from the
     config.  It was 100.0 N*m, which gave this platform a 600 N*m / 3371 N
     ceiling - the largest of the three, and the reason its trouble on grades
     was never a torque-ceiling problem.

     SWEPT BY HAND 2026-09-06 to 2026-09-08: 20.0 -> 15.0 -> 10.0 -> 20.0
     N*m per wheel, i.e. back to where it started.  This platform is the
     ANCHOR of the budget: the figure here is chosen and the other two are
     solved from it (Husky 29.958 -> 22.468 -> 14.979 -> 29.958, Tracked
     60.0 -> 45.0 -> 30.0 -> 60.0), which is why moving it by hand means
     editing sim_config.yaml too.
     20.0 N*m per wheel is ~5.3x the 3.76 N*m the 16.7 deg ramp demands of
     each of the six.

     damping and friction are 0.0 for the same parity reason: they used to be
     0.5 and 0.1 here and absent on the other two platforms, i.e. 7.4 N*m of
     drag (a third of the ramp torque) that only this platform paid.  Joint
     drag is not a locomotion property being compared; contact friction, which
     is shared, is.  The PASSIVE suspension pivots and the steering joints keep
     their damping - they model real hardware and have no counterpart to match.
     ========================================================== -->

<joint name="rev_9" type="continuous">
  <origin xyz="${(0)+(0)+(0)} ${(0.0000000)+(-0.0218551)+(-0.0153792)} ${(rk_p79)+(rk_p58)+(0)}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="BarraRuedas_3_1"/>
  <child link="Rueda_2_1"/>
  <axis xyz="0.0 1.0 0.0"/>
  <limit effort="20.0" velocity="5.0"/>
  <dynamics damping="0.0" friction="0.0"/>
</joint>

<joint name="rev_10" type="continuous">
  <origin xyz="${(-0.0509408)+(-0.0828865)+(-0.0293571)+(0)+(0)+(0)} ${(-0.0161893)+(0.0000000)+(0.0000000)+(0.0000000)+(-0.0218551)+(-0.0153792)} ${(-0.053192 + bogie_drop)+(-0.086551)+(rk_p74)+(rk_p79)+(rk_p58)+(0)}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="Acople_Rodamiento_1_1"/>
  <child link="Rueda_4_1"/>
  <axis xyz="0.0 1.0 0.0"/>
  <limit effort="20.0" velocity="5.0"/>
  <dynamics damping="0.0" friction="0.0"/>
</joint>

<joint name="rev_11" type="continuous">
  <origin xyz="${(0)+(0)+(0)} ${(0.0000000)+(-0.0218551)+(-0.0153792)} ${(rk_p79)+(rk_p58)+(0)}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="BarraRuedas_1_1"/>
  <child link="Rueda_6_1"/>
  <axis xyz="0.0 1.0 0.0"/>
  <limit effort="20.0" velocity="5.0"/>
  <dynamics damping="0.0" friction="0.0"/>
</joint>

<joint name="rev_12" type="continuous">
  <origin xyz="${(0)+(0)+(0)} ${(0.0000000)+(0.0218551)+(0.0153792)} ${(rk_p79)+(rk_p58)+(0)}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="BarraRuedas_4_1"/>
  <child link="Rueda_1_1"/>
  <axis xyz="-0.0 1.0 -0.0"/>
  <limit effort="20.0" velocity="5.0"/>
  <dynamics damping="0.0" friction="0.0"/>
</joint>

<joint name="rev_13" type="continuous">
  <origin xyz="${(-0.0509408)+(-0.0828865)+(-0.0293571)+(0)+(0)+(0)} ${(0.0161893)+(0.0000000)+(0.0000000)+(0.0000000)+(0.0218551)+(0.0153792)} ${(-0.053192 + bogie_drop)+(-0.086551)+(rk_p74)+(rk_p79)+(rk_p58)+(0)}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="Acople_Rodamiento_2_1"/>
  <child link="Rueda_3_1"/>
  <axis xyz="-0.0 1.0 -0.0"/>
  <limit effort="20.0" velocity="5.0"/>
  <dynamics damping="0.0" friction="0.0"/>
</joint>

<joint name="rev_14" type="continuous">
  <origin xyz="${(0)+(0)+(0)} ${(0.0000000)+(0.0218551)+(0.0153792)} ${(rk_p79)+(rk_p58)+(0)}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <parent link="BarraRuedas_6_1"/>
  <child link="Rueda_5_1"/>
  <axis xyz="-0.0 1.0 0.0"/>
  <limit effort="20.0" velocity="5.0"/>
  <dynamics damping="0.0" friction="0.0"/>
</joint>

<!-- rev_1 and rev_2 removed: AcopleTuboRB now connects to base_link via differential_joint -->
<!-- Rocker pivots are now rocker_joint_r and rocker_joint_l -->

<!-- ================= SENSOR LINKS ================= -->

<!-- Velodyne LiDAR base link -->
<link name="velodyne_base_link">
  <inertial>
    <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <mass value="0.825345"/>
    <inertia ixx="0.000508103703" iyy="0.000508103703" izz="0.000618055477" ixy="0" iyz="0" ixz="0"/>
  </inertial>
  <visual>
    <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <cylinder radius="0.0387" length="0.0538"/>
    </geometry>
    <material name="dark_grey">
      <color rgba="0.1 0.1 0.1 1.0"/>
    </material>
  </visual>
  <collision>
    <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <cylinder radius="0.0387" length="0.0538"/>
    </geometry>
  </collision>
</link>

<joint name="velodyne_base_mount_joint" type="fixed">
  <!-- SENSOR STANDARDIZATION.
         z = 0.6700 + (-0.1232) - 0.0641 = 0.4827
       0.6700 m is the ray-origin height above the wheel contact plane shared
       by the three platforms, -0.1232 m is this platform's contact plane in
       base_link, and 0.0641 m is the Velodyne's own <sensor><pose> over in
       ensamblajeurdf.gazebo.  See robot_metrics/README.md section 1.5.

       RAISED FROM 0.6375 (= 0.8248 m of height) on 2026-08-26.  At the old
       height this robot blocked its own lowest ring by 6.8 deg towards the
       camera, 3.9 deg at the chassis corners and 1.7 deg over the rocker
       tubes - 3.3% of the sphere returning nothing.  The monolithic gpu_ray
       never saw any of it: Gazebo does not let a ray sensor see the visual of
       its own link, and the URDF conversion fuses velodyne_base_link into
       base_link, so the sensor was ignoring the entire chassis.  The sliced
       LiDAR's wedge rides on velodyne_rotor_link, a link of its own, so the
       robot becomes a normal obstacle to it and those rings disappear.  At
       0.9000 m the -15 deg ring clears everything by 4.2 deg. -->
  <origin xyz="0.244209 0.100343 ${0.5127 + rocker_drop}" rpy="0 0 3.141592653589793"/>
  <parent link="chassis_link"/>
  <child link="velodyne_base_link"/>
</joint>

<!-- IMU link -->
<link name="imu_link">
  <inertial>
    <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <mass value="0.0149159"/>
    <inertia ixx="1.73667336e-06" iyy="2.43716141e-06" izz="3.61448852e-06" ixy="0" iyz="0" ixz="0"/>
  </inertial>
  <visual>
    <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <box size="0.041662 0.034237 0.015"/>
    </geometry>
    <material name="dark_grey"/>
  </visual>
  <collision>
    <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <box size="0.041662 0.034237 0.015"/>
    </geometry>
  </collision>
</link>

<joint name="imu_joint" type="fixed">
  <!-- SENSOR STANDARDIZATION: el IMU va en el CENTRO DE MASAS.  Cambiado
       2026-09-04; el razonamiento completo esta en
       differential/model/differential/model.sdf.  Antes: "0.283305 0.116408
       0.57375", que lo dejaba a 0.6969 m del suelo como los otros dos pero a
       0.238 m del CM, contra 0.485 m del tracked.
       CM del rocker sin el IMU, en base_link.  Ojo: este chasis tiene
       suspension articulada, asi que su CM se mueve con los balancines; este
       es el de la pose nominal, con las juntas a cero.

       Y SE MUEVE TAMBIEN CON bogie_drop.  Bajar el pivote se lleva abajo los
       dos soportes Acople_Rodamiento_*_1 (1.39525 kg nativos cada uno), que
       es la unica masa que cambia de sitio.  El CM sin el IMU baja
           dz = 2 * 1.39525 * mass_scale * bogie_drop / (45.0 - m_imu)
              = 0.029419 * bogie_drop
       o sea 10.3 mm con bogie_drop = 0.351.  Eso es CINCO VECES la tolerancia
       de imu_lever_arm_tolerance_m (0.002) de sim_config.yaml, asi que si el
       IMU no baja con el CM, check_sim_parity 13b (check_imu_at_centre_of_mass)
       FALLA, y con razon: un acelerometro fuera del CM mide
       a_cm + alpha x r + omega x (omega x r), y ese brazo es justo lo que la
       estandarizacion de sensores puso a cero el 2026-09-04.
       x e y no se tocan: la bajada del pivote es puramente vertical. -->
  <xacro:property name="imu_z_drop"
                  value="${2*1.39525*mass_scale*bogie_drop / (45.0 - 0.0149159)}"/>
  <origin xyz="0.248647 0.100343 0.474405" rpy="0 0 3.141592653589793"/>
  <parent link="chassis_link"/>
  <child link="imu_link"/>
</joint>

<!-- Camera link -->
<link name="camera_link">
  <inertial>
    <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <mass value="0.00994392"/>
    <inertia ixx="2.90619216e-06" iyy="9.37297333e-07" izz="3.00447124e-06" ixy="0" iyz="0" ixz="0"/>
  </inertial>
  <visual>
    <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <box size="0.024997 0.05478 0.0225"/>
    </geometry>
    <material name="dark_grey"/>
  </visual>
  <collision>
    <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <geometry>
      <box size="0.024997 0.05478 0.0225"/>
    </geometry>
  </collision>
</link>

<joint name="camera_joint" type="fixed">
  <origin xyz="-0.110091 0.100343 ${0.4375 + rocker_drop}" rpy="0 0 3.141592653589793"/>
  <parent link="chassis_link"/>
  <child link="camera_link"/>
</joint>

<!-- Camera optical frame -->
<link name="camera_optical_frame">
  <inertial>
    <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <mass value="0.000994392"/>
    <inertia ixx="9.94392e-06" iyy="9.94392e-06" izz="9.94392e-06" ixy="0" iyz="0" ixz="0"/>
  </inertial>
</link>

<joint name="camera_optical_joint" type="fixed">
  <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="-1.5707963267948966 0 -1.5707963267948966"/>
  <parent link="camera_link"/>
  <child link="camera_optical_frame"/>
</joint>

<!-- ============== GEOMETRIC ROCKER DIFFERENTIAL ==============
  The differential as a mechanism rather than as a constraint equation: a beam
  pivoted on the chassis about the ROLL axis, with a vertical rod from each of
  its ends down to the corresponding rocker.  Left rocker pitches nose-up ->
  its rod pushes the beam's left end up -> the beam rolls -> the right end goes
  down -> right rocker pitches nose-down.  That is phi_left = -phi_right,
  obtained from geometry instead of from a sign convention, which is the reason
  for building it: the gearbox version imposes the same relation exactly but
  leaves the resulting roll freedom unrestrained, and its sign had to be argued
  rather than seen.  This is also what the Curio / Sawppy rover does.

  GEOMETRY IS SYNTHESISED, NOT FROM CAD - but it is anchored on links that
  exist.  The first version of this attached the rods 0.20 m forward of each
  rocker pivot and hung the beam 0.30 m above them, which put the whole linkage
  at z = 0.877 when the highest point on the robot is z = 0.664: it floated
  clear of the vehicle, joined to nothing you could see.  It still worked,
  because a joint frame is defined relative to a link ORIGIN and not to where
  that link's mesh happens to be - which is exactly how a linkage can be
  kinematically right and physically nonsense at the same time.

  The attachment points are now measured, not assumed.  BarraBR_2_1 and
  BarraBR_1_1 are the rocker bars: real, drawn, and DIRECT fixed children of the
  two rocker links, so they move with the rocker and with nothing else.  Their
  joint origins in the rocker frame are

      right  BarraBR_2_1   (-0.059096, +0.023282, -0.053192)
      left   BarraBR_1_1   (-0.059096, -0.023282, -0.053192)

  which in base_link are (0.2242, +0.3085, 0.5235) and (0.2242, -0.0757,
  0.5235).  Their midpoint in y is 0.11640 - the centreline, where the CoG also
  sits - so the beam is symmetric about the vehicle with a half-span of 0.19210
  and its ends land directly over the two attachment points.  The beam sits
  0.15 m above them at z = 0.6735, just clear of the robot's 0.664 m top, and
  the rods are 0.15 m and vertical.

  Rods vertical by construction, so rocker vertical travel maps to beam vertical
  travel one-for-one and the ratio is exactly 1.

  THE LEVER ARM IS SHORT: 0.0591 m from the rocker pivot to the attachment,
  because that is where the rocker structure actually is.  The forces in the
  linkage are therefore about 3.4x what the earlier 0.20 m arm would have
  carried.  That is the real mechanism's geometry, not a modelling choice, but
  it does mean the rods are lightly loaded bodies carrying large forces - watch
  for solver noise.

  NO COLLISION GEOMETRY on any of these three links, deliberately: they are
  stand-ins for a linkage whose real shape is unknown, and giving them collision
  bodies would invent interference that the real mechanism may not have.  They
  carry inertia and visuals only.  Their 0.15 kg is taken off base_link above so
  the 45.000 kg total is unchanged.

  The far ends are joined to the rockers by BALL joints declared in <gazebo>
  blocks at the bottom of this file - that is the loop closure, and it is what
  URDF cannot express on its own.
  =========================================================== -->
<xacro:if value="$(arg differential_linkage)">

  <link name="differential_beam">
    <inertial>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <mass value="${0.05*mass_scale}"/>
      <!-- barra fina a lo largo de y.  OJO: estos ixx/izz son de un
           largo de 0.384 m y el cilindro mide 0.471546, asi que la inercia
           esta un 24 % corta.  Se deja porque el cuerpo pesa 0.024 kg
           -es un sustituto de un varillaje cuya forma real no se conoce- y
           el efecto es despreciable; anotado para que no engañe. -->
      <inertia ixx="${0.000615*mass_scale}" iyy="${0.0000025*mass_scale}"
               izz="${0.000615*mass_scale}" ixy="0" iyz="0" ixz="0"/>
    </inertial>
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="1.5707963 0 0"/>
      <geometry><cylinder radius="0.010" length="0.471546"/></geometry>
      <material name="silver"/>
    </visual>
  </link>

  <joint name="differential_beam_joint" type="revolute">
    <!-- ENCIMA DEL CHASIS en las dos variantes, en z 0.673472, que son 38.5
         mm por encima del techo (0.635).  x el del pivote del bogie
         (0.244209 + 0.163184) y la linea central del vehiculo.

         En la variante longitudinal la viga no baja a buscar el enganche:
         es el ROCKER el que sube a buscarla, con un brazo vertical de
         96.808 mm desde su eje (0.576664 + 0.096808 = 0.673472).  Asi la
         barra sale horizontal SIN meter el varillaje en la panza, que es lo
         que pasaba cuando la viga bajaba a 0.523472. -->
    <origin xyz="0.407393 0.100344 0.673472" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    <parent link="chassis_link"/>
    <child link="differential_beam"/>
    <!-- longitudinal: eje VERTICAL, los extremos barren en +-x y empujan las
         barras.  transversal: eje de BALANCEO, los extremos suben y bajan. -->
    <axis xyz="${'0 0 1' if dlong else '1 0 0'}"/>
    <!-- TOPE +-0.7 en las dos, y por la misma cuenta.  Longitudinal: la
         barra iguala avances, y_off*sin(psi) = brazo*sin(theta), asi que con
         el rocker en su tope de 1.0 rad la viga gira
         asin(0.096808*sin(1.0)/0.1335592) = 0.656 rad.
         Transversal: asin(0.163184*sin(1.0)/0.225773) = 0.653 rad.
         Las dos se quedan justo por debajo de 0.7, asi que la viga no hace
         de tope antes que el propio rocker. -->
    <limit lower="-0.7" upper="0.7" effort="1000" velocity="10.0"/>
    <dynamics damping="0.1" friction="0.05"/>
  </joint>

  <!-- LA BARRA.
       Longitudinal: horizontal y a lo largo del vehiculo, de la viga a la
       punta del brazo del rocker, que esta 96.808 mm ENCIMA de su eje y en
       su misma x.  Largo = 0.407393 - 0.244209 = 0.163184, y la junta de
       lleva pitch pi/2 para tumbar el cilindro sobre x.  La barra trabaja a
       traccion y compresion: iguala el avance del extremo de la viga con el
       del enganche, y de esa igualdad sale theta_izq + theta_der = 0.
       Transversal (la de antes): baja inclinada hasta el soporte de BarraBR
       (link_r_5/6), 50.9 mm por delante del eje del rocker, con
         dx = 0.1631835 + 0.0509408
         dz = 0.673472 - (0.576664 - 0.053192) = 0.150000
       o sea pitch atan2(dx, dz) = 0.9597 rad (55 deg) y 0.261436 m. -->
  <xacro:property name="diff_rod_dx" value="${0.1631835 + 0.0509408}"/>
  <xacro:property name="diff_rod_len"
                  value="${0.1631835 if dlong else sqrt(diff_rod_dx**2 + 0.15**2)}"/>
  <xacro:property name="diff_rod_pitch"
                  value="${1.5707963267948966 if dlong else atan2(diff_rod_dx, 0.15)}"/>
  <xacro:macro name="diff_rod" params="side y_off">
    <link name="diff_rod_${side}">
      <inertial>
        <origin xyz="0 0 ${-diff_rod_len/2}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <mass value="${0.05*mass_scale}"/>
        <inertia ixx="${0.0000938*mass_scale}" iyy="${0.0000938*mass_scale}"
                 izz="${0.0000016*mass_scale}" ixy="0" iyz="0" ixz="0"/>
      </inertial>
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="0 0 ${-diff_rod_len/2}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <geometry><cylinder radius="0.008" length="${diff_rod_len}"/></geometry>
        <material name="silver"/>
      </visual>
    </link>
    <joint name="diff_rod_${side}_joint" type="revolute">
      <origin xyz="0 ${y_off} 0" rpy="0 ${diff_rod_pitch} 0"/>
      <parent link="differential_beam"/>
      <child link="diff_rod_${side}"/>
      <!-- EL AXIS VA EN EL MARCO DEL HIJO, y el hijo lleva el pitch de
           arriba.  Longitudinal: la barra gira con la viga en el plano
           horizontal, o sea sobre la Z del padre, que con pitch pi/2 es la
           -X del hijo.  Transversal: bascula en el plano x-z, sobre la Y,
           que el pitch no mueve. -->
      <axis xyz="${'-1 0 0' if dlong else '0 1 0'}"/>
      <limit lower="-1.2" upper="1.2" effort="1000" velocity="10.0"/>
      <dynamics damping="0.05" friction="0.02"/>
    </joint>
  </xacro:macro>

  <xacro:diff_rod side="left"  y_off="-0.1335592"/>
  <xacro:diff_rod side="right" y_off="0.1335592"/>

  <!-- LOOP CLOSURE.  SDFormat joints inside <gazebo> are added to the model
       after the URDF is converted, which is how a closed chain is expressed
       from URDF at all.  Ball joints rather than revolute: the rod already has
       a revolute at its top, and a second revolute would over-constrain the
       loop against the 0.2 mm of asymmetry in the synthesised geometry.  The
       pose is the attachment point in the CHILD (rocker) link frame. -->
  <!-- DONDE CIERRA, en el marco del tubo del rocker.
       Longitudinal: (0, -+0.0161893, +0.096808), o sea en la PUNTA DEL
       BRAZO, justo ENCIMA del eje del rocker y a la altura de la viga.  Con
       dx = 0 el enganche se mueve solo en x al cabecear el rocker
       (brazo*theta) y nada en z a primer orden, que es lo que necesita una
       barra horizontal.  El brazo pasa a ser 96.808 mm: mas que los 73.7 de
       la transversal, asi que las barras van MENOS cargadas, pero tambien
       mas cerca de los 163.2 que estrangularon el modo diferencial en drop5.
       Es lo que hay que vigilar en la primera corrida.
       Transversal: (-0.0509408, ...), el soporte de BarraBR (link_r_5/6),
       50.9 mm por delante del eje.  Vuelto ahi el 2026-09-13 desde el pivote
       del bogie (brazo 163.2 mm), porque en drop5 el brazo largo estrangulo
       el modo diferencial; es el brazo con el que se midieron drop3 y drop4.
       Junta de BOLA y no revolute: la barra ya lleva una revolute arriba, y
       una segunda sobreconstriñe el lazo contra los 0.2 mm de asimetria de
       la geometria sintetizada. -->
  <xacro:property name="diff_close_x" value="${0.0 if dlong else -0.0509408}"/>
  <xacro:property name="diff_close_z"
                  value="${0.096808 if dlong else -0.053192}"/>
  <gazebo>
    <joint name="diff_close_left" type="ball">
      <pose>${diff_close_x} -0.0161893 ${diff_close_z} 0 0 0</pose>
      <parent>diff_rod_left</parent>
      <child>AcopleTuboRB_2_1</child>
    </joint>
  </gazebo>
  <gazebo>
    <joint name="diff_close_right" type="ball">
      <pose>${diff_close_x} 0.0161893 ${diff_close_z} 0 0 0</pose>
      <parent>diff_rod_right</parent>
      <child>AcopleTuboRB_1_1</child>
    </joint>
  </gazebo>

</xacro:if>

</robot>
